{"qid": 0, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "The implied warranty of merchantability requires goods sold by a merchant to be fit for their ordinary purposes. An item that is defective, unsafe, and unfit for ordinary use breaches that warranty, assuming it has not been validly disclaimed.\nFINAL: Implied warranty of merchantability.", "final": "Implied warranty of merchantability."}
{"qid": 1, "answered": "true_answer", "resp": "A merchant implicitly warrants that the goods sold are fit for their ordinary purposes. Defective and unsafe goods breach this implied warranty, assuming it has not been validly disclaimed.\nFINAL: Implied warranty of merchantability.", "final": "Implied warranty of merchantability."}
{"qid": 2, "answered": null, "resp": "The strongest argument is that the state is acting as a market participant by purchasing construction services with its own funds, rather than regulating the construction market generally. The market-participant exception permits a state to favor its own residents in such transactions without violating the dormant Commerce Clause.\nFINAL: The state is spending its own funds on construction contracts and therefore falls within the market-participant exception to the dormant Commerce Clause.", "final": "The state is spending its own funds on construction contracts and therefore falls within the market-participant exception to the dormant Commerce Clause."}
{"qid": 3, "answered": null, "resp": "The Commerce Clause generally permits a state acting as a market participant, rather than as a market regulator, to favor its own citizens. Thus, the strongest supporting fact is that the state is purchasing construction services with its own funds.\nFINAL: The state is acting as a market participant by funding the construction contracts itself, not as a market regulator.", "final": "The state is acting as a market participant by funding the construction contracts itself, not as a market regulator."}
{"qid": 4, "answered": null, "resp": "Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 32(a)(3), an adverse party may use a party\u2019s deposition for any purpose, regardless of whether the deponent is available to testify. Thus, Cooper may read admissible portions of March\u2019s deposition at trial without calling March to the stand.\n\nFINAL: The court will overrule March\u2019s objection and permit Cooper to use the deposition.", "final": "The court will overrule March\u2019s objection and permit Cooper to use the deposition."}
