{"qid": 54, "answered": null, "resp": "Under U.S. law, a valid treaty and a federal statute have equal rank, and courts try to interpret them consistently. If they irreconcilably conflict, the later-enacted measure controls domestically, though overriding a treaty does not eliminate the United States\u2019 international obligations.\n\nFINAL: The later-in-time rule applies: whichever was adopted later\u2014the congressional act or the treaty\u2014prevails under domestic law.", "final": "The later-in-time rule applies: whichever was adopted later\u2014the congressional act or the treaty\u2014prevails under domestic law."}
