{"qid": 109, "answered": null, "resp": "Verbal stimuli are not innate drives like hunger or thirst, but words associated with rewards, threats, or painful experiences can acquire motivational properties through learning. For example, \u201cfailure\u201d or \u201cpunishment\u201d may evoke anxiety and motivate avoidance, while words of praise may encourage approach and effort.\n\nFINAL: Verbal stimuli may become an acquired source of drive through conditioning and learned meaning: they can arouse emotional tension and motivate behaviour aimed at reducing it. Their effects depend on personal experience and social context; strictly speaking, they are learned cues that evoke motivational states rather than biological drives themselves.", "final": "Verbal stimuli may become an acquired source of drive through conditioning and learned meaning: they can arouse emotional tension and motivate behaviour aimed at reducing it. Their effects depend on personal experience and social context; strictly speaking, they are learned cues that evoke motivational states rather than biological drives themselves."}
{"qid": 110, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: Adams\u2019s equity theory states that employees\u2019 motivation depends on perceived fairness, assessed by comparing their outcome-to-input ratio\u2014rewards such as pay, recognition, and promotion relative to contributions such as effort, skill, and experience\u2014with that of a relevant other. Equity is perceived when these ratios are equal; inequity is perceived when one\u2019s ratio is lower (under-reward) or higher (over-reward), even if absolute rewards differ. Perceived inequity creates tension and may prompt individuals to change their effort, seek different rewards, reinterpret the comparison, choose another reference person, or leave the situation.", "final": "Adams\u2019s equity theory states that employees\u2019 motivation depends on perceived fairness, assessed by comparing their outcome-to-input ratio\u2014rewards such as pay, recognition, and promotion relative to contributions such as effort, skill, and experience\u2014with that of a relevant other. Equity is perceived when these ratios are equal; inequity is perceived when one\u2019s ratio is lower (under-reward) or higher (over-reward), even if absolute rewards differ. Perceived inequity creates tension and may prompt individuals to change their effort, seek different rewards, reinterpret the comparison, choose another reference person, or leave the situation."}
