{"qid": 225, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: Four common types of reliability in measurement are:\n1. **Test\u2013retest reliability:** The consistency of scores when the same test is administered to the same people at different times.\n2. **Inter-rater reliability:** The degree of agreement between different observers or raters assessing the same behavior or performance.\n3. **Parallel-forms reliability:** The consistency of scores across two equivalent versions of a test measuring the same construct.\n4. **Internal consistency reliability:** The extent to which items within a test consistently measure the same construct, often assessed using Cronbach\u2019s alpha or split-half reliability.", "final": "Four common types of reliability in measurement are:"}
{"qid": 226, "answered": null, "resp": "Helmholtz\u2019s rule says that we unconsciously interpret sensory information as arising from the most likely cause, based on past experience; illusions occur when this normally useful inference is wrong. Similarly, we interpret our internal sensations using context and expectations, so the same racing heart might be experienced as fear, excitement, or attraction.\n\nFINAL: Illusions and interpretations of our feelings both reflect unconscious inferences about the likely causes of sensations\u2014interpretations that are usually helpful but can be mistaken.", "final": "Illusions and interpretations of our feelings both reflect unconscious inferences about the likely causes of sensations\u2014interpretations that are usually helpful but can be mistaken."}
{"qid": 227, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: Johnson proposed three stages of problem solving:\n1. **Preparation:** Recognize and define the problem, gather relevant information, and identify the requirements of a solution.\n2. **Production:** Generate possible solutions by drawing on prior knowledge, experience, and creative thinking.\n3. **Judgment:** Evaluate and test the proposed solutions, selecting the one that best resolves the problem.", "final": "Johnson proposed three stages of problem solving:"}
