{"qid": 233, "answered": null, "resp": "Findings on Rotter\u2019s Internal\u2013External (I\u2013E) Control Scale concern average beliefs about whether outcomes depend on one\u2019s own actions (internal control) or on luck, fate, and powerful others (external control). Reported differences vary across samples and social circumstances.\n\nFINAL: **Sex differences are generally small and inconsistent**: many studies find no significant difference, while some find women somewhat more external than men. **Racial differences have been reported more often**, with Black Americans in some U.S. studies scoring more externally than White Americans, although this is not universal and is influenced by socioeconomic status, education, and experiences of discrimination. Such findings should not be interpreted as innate characteristics or as descriptions of every individual; greater externality may realistically reflect restricted opportunities and less actual control over outcomes. The scale measures generalized expectations of control, not ability or personal worth.", "final": "**Sex differences are generally small and inconsistent**: many studies find no significant difference, while some find women somewhat more external than men. **Racial differences have been reported more often**, with Black Americans in some U.S. studies scoring more externally than White Americans, although this is not universal and is influenced by socioeconomic status, education, and experiences of discrimination. Such findings should not be interpreted as innate characteristics or as descriptions of every individual; greater externality may realistically reflect restricted opportunities and less actual control over outcomes. The scale measures generalized expectations of control, not ability or personal worth."}
