{"qid": 267, "answered": null, "resp": "Freud\u2019s psychoanalytic theory was historically influential, but many of its central claims lack strong scientific support.\n\nFINAL: Major criticisms include that its concepts are often difficult to test or falsify; it relied heavily on a small, unrepresentative set of clinical cases and subjective interpretations; and it overemphasized unconscious sexual and aggressive drives and early childhood experiences while underestimating social, cultural, and biological influences. Critics also regard ideas such as penis envy and the Oedipus complex as culturally biased or insufficiently supported, and argue that its deterministic view gives too little weight to human agency and development throughout life.", "final": "Major criticisms include that its concepts are often difficult to test or falsify; it relied heavily on a small, unrepresentative set of clinical cases and subjective interpretations; and it overemphasized unconscious sexual and aggressive drives and early childhood experiences while underestimating social, cultural, and biological influences. Critics also regard ideas such as penis envy and the Oedipus complex as culturally biased or insufficiently supported, and argue that its deterministic view gives too little weight to human agency and development throughout life."}
