{"qid": 279, "answered": null, "resp": "Selecting a manager requires assessing job-related competence, leadership, judgment, and interpersonal skills through consistent, evidence-based methods. Using several complementary assessments is more reliable and fairer than relying solely on qualifications or an unstructured interview.\nFINAL: I would use the following selection procedure: (1) **Define the role and selection criteria** through job analysis, identifying required experience, technical knowledge, leadership skills, and performance expectations; (2) **Screen applications** against these criteria to shortlist qualified candidates; (3) **Conduct structured interviews**, asking all candidates comparable behavioral and situational questions about delegation, conflict resolution, decision-making, and team development; (4) **Use job-relevant work samples or an assessment center**, such as a business case, an in-tray exercise, or a coaching role-play, to observe managerial performance directly; (5) **Add validated ability or personality assessments where appropriate**, using them as supporting evidence rather than the sole basis for selection; (6) **Check references and verify qualifications**, with consent and in accordance with applicable law; and (7) **Make and document the decision** using predefined scoring criteria, trained assessors, and reasonable accommodations to ensure fairness, then offer the position and establish clear onboarding and performance-review goals.", "final": "I would use the following selection procedure: (1) **Define the role and selection criteria** through job analysis, identifying required experience, technical knowledge, leadership skills, and performance expectations; (2) **Screen applications** against these criteria to shortlist qualified candidates; (3) **Conduct structured interviews**, asking all candidates comparable behavioral and situational questions about delegation, conflict resolution, decision-making, and team development; (4) **Use job-relevant work samples or an assessment center**, such as a business case, an in-tray exercise, or a coaching role-play, to observe managerial performance directly; (5) **Add validated ability or personality assessments where appropriate**, using them as supporting evidence rather than the sole basis for selection; (6) **Check references and verify qualifications**, with consent and in accordance with applicable law; and (7) **Make and document the decision** using predefined scoring criteria, trained assessors, and reasonable accommodations to ensure fairness, then offer the position and establish clear onboarding and performance-review goals."}
{"qid": 280, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: Criterion-related validity is the extent to which a test\u2019s scores correspond to an external measure of the outcome or characteristic being assessed. It includes concurrent validity (agreement with a criterion measured at the same time) and predictive validity (accuracy in predicting a future outcome).", "final": "Criterion-related validity is the extent to which a test\u2019s scores correspond to an external measure of the outcome or characteristic being assessed. It includes concurrent validity (agreement with a criterion measured at the same time) and predictive validity (accuracy in predicting a future outcome)."}
