{"qid": 326, "answered": null, "resp": "The American Psychiatric Association\u2019s *Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders* (DSM) provides a common diagnostic language, but its categories do not always capture the complexity or context of behavior.\n\nFINAL: Critics argue that DSM categories can have subjective boundaries and overlapping symptoms, leading to inconsistent diagnoses; treat disorders as discrete categories when difficulties often occur along a continuum; give insufficient weight to cultural, developmental, and environmental factors; and pathologize normal variations in behavior. Diagnostic labels may also stigmatize people, obscure individual differences, and describe symptoms without necessarily explaining their causes or identifying the best treatment.", "final": "Critics argue that DSM categories can have subjective boundaries and overlapping symptoms, leading to inconsistent diagnoses; treat disorders as discrete categories when difficulties often occur along a continuum; give insufficient weight to cultural, developmental, and environmental factors; and pathologize normal variations in behavior. Diagnostic labels may also stigmatize people, obscure individual differences, and describe symptoms without necessarily explaining their causes or identifying the best treatment."}
{"qid": 327, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: In individuals, Achievement Motive (nAch) is traditionally measured using the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), scoring stories written about ambiguous pictures for achievement imagery, such as striving to meet standards of excellence. In countries, it is estimated by applying similar content analysis to cultural materials\u2014especially children\u2019s school readers\u2014and calculating the frequency of achievement themes as an index of national achievement motivation.", "final": "In individuals, Achievement Motive (nAch) is traditionally measured using the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), scoring stories written about ambiguous pictures for achievement imagery, such as striving to meet standards of excellence. In countries, it is estimated by applying similar content analysis to cultural materials\u2014especially children\u2019s school readers\u2014and calculating the frequency of achievement themes as an index of national achievement motivation."}
{"qid": 328, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: An avoidance-avoidance conflict occurs when a person must choose between two undesirable alternatives. It can produce **vacillation**, or indecisively shifting between the alternatives as each seems worse when approached, and **escape or withdrawal**, in which the person tries to avoid the decision or leave the situation altogether.", "final": "An avoidance-avoidance conflict occurs when a person must choose between two undesirable alternatives. It can produce **vacillation**, or indecisively shifting between the alternatives as each seems worse when approached, and **escape or withdrawal**, in which the person tries to avoid the decision or leave the situation altogether."}
{"qid": 329, "answered": null, "resp": "Twin and adoption studies suggest that genetic differences account for roughly 40\u201360% of variation in major personality traits within studied populations, influencing tendencies such as emotional reactivity, sociability, and self-control. These influences involve many genes and interact with upbringing, culture, and individual experiences, so heritability does not imply that personality is fixed or genetically predetermined.\nFINAL: Heredity provides a substantial biological predisposition toward certain personality traits, but personality develops through the continuing interplay of genes and environment.", "final": "Heredity provides a substantial biological predisposition toward certain personality traits, but personality develops through the continuing interplay of genes and environment."}
{"qid": 330, "answered": null, "resp": "Smell perception varies with biological sensitivity, the condition of our olfactory system, and how our brains interpret familiar odors.\nFINAL: Three factors are genetic makeup, age and health, and prior experiences and learned associations with odors.", "final": "Three factors are genetic makeup, age and health, and prior experiences and learned associations with odors."}
{"qid": 331, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: Maturation is the orderly process of biological growth and development, guided largely by genetic factors rather than learning alone. It provides the foundation for much human behavior by developing the brain, nervous system, and body, making abilities such as walking, speaking, and regulating emotions possible as the individual becomes ready; experience and learning then shape how these abilities develop and are expressed.", "final": "Maturation is the orderly process of biological growth and development, guided largely by genetic factors rather than learning alone. It provides the foundation for much human behavior by developing the brain, nervous system, and body, making abilities such as walking, speaking, and regulating emotions possible as the individual becomes ready; experience and learning then shape how these abilities develop and are expressed."}
{"qid": 332, "answered": null, "resp": "Evaluation apprehension arises when participants worry that researchers are judging them, potentially altering their behavior. It cannot reliably be eliminated entirely, but it can be minimized by ensuring anonymity, emphasizing that there are no right or wrong responses, and reducing observation or using unobtrusive measures.\nFINAL: Minimize evaluation apprehension through anonymous responses, nonjudgmental instructions, and unobtrusive observation; complete elimination cannot be guaranteed.", "final": "Minimize evaluation apprehension through anonymous responses, nonjudgmental instructions, and unobtrusive observation; complete elimination cannot be guaranteed."}
{"qid": 333, "answered": null, "resp": "Studies show that during a tip-of-the-tongue state, people can often recall a word\u2019s meaning and some features\u2014such as its first letter, number of syllables, or stress pattern\u2014without retrieving the whole word. This suggests that access to a word\u2019s meaning can occur separately from access to its sound.\nFINAL: TOT studies find that temporarily inaccessible words remain partly available in memory: people often accurately report their meaning and fragments of their sound, recognize them when presented, and eventually retrieve them, sometimes with phonological cues.", "final": "TOT studies find that temporarily inaccessible words remain partly available in memory: people often accurately report their meaning and fragments of their sound, recognize them when presented, and eventually retrieve them, sometimes with phonological cues."}
{"qid": 334, "answered": null, "resp": "LSD\u2019s effects do not faithfully reproduce psychotic illnesses such as schizophrenia: LSD typically causes visual distortions with some awareness that they are drug-induced, whereas schizophrenia more often involves auditory hallucinations, delusions, and impaired reality testing. Some symptoms overlap, but that does not make the two states equivalent.\nFINAL: The \u201cpsychotomimetic\u201d explanation was rejected as an oversimplification because LSD\u2019s perceptual and psychological effects differ substantially from those of naturally occurring psychosis.", "final": "The \u201cpsychotomimetic\u201d explanation was rejected as an oversimplification because LSD\u2019s perceptual and psychological effects differ substantially from those of naturally occurring psychosis."}
{"qid": 335, "answered": null, "resp": "Cross-cultural tests are designed to minimize the effects of language, education, and culture so that abilities can be assessed more fairly across cultural groups. They typically use nonverbal instructions, pictures, patterns, or performance tasks, although no test is completely culture-free.\n\nFINAL: **Characteristics:** minimal language demands, reduced reliance on culturally specific knowledge and schooling, emphasis on nonverbal reasoning or performance, and validation for the cultural groups being assessed. **Four examples:** Raven\u2019s Progressive Matrices, Cattell\u2019s Culture Fair Intelligence Test, the Leiter International Performance Scale, and the Goodenough\u2013Harris Draw-a-Person Test.", "final": "**Characteristics:** minimal language demands, reduced reliance on culturally specific knowledge and schooling, emphasis on nonverbal reasoning or performance, and validation for the cultural groups being assessed. **Four examples:** Raven\u2019s Progressive Matrices, Cattell\u2019s Culture Fair Intelligence Test, the Leiter International Performance Scale, and the Goodenough\u2013Harris Draw-a-Person Test."}
{"qid": 336, "answered": null, "resp": "Attribution theory examines how people explain behavior, while stereotyping examines how beliefs about social groups shape judgments of individuals. They intersect when stereotypes bias causal explanations\u2014for example, attributing an out-group member\u2019s negative behavior to character but positive behavior to circumstances.\n\nFINAL: The study of stereotyping relates to attribution theory by showing how group-based beliefs bias whether people explain behavior through personal traits or situational factors, often reinforcing existing stereotypes.", "final": "The study of stereotyping relates to attribution theory by showing how group-based beliefs bias whether people explain behavior through personal traits or situational factors, often reinforcing existing stereotypes."}
{"qid": 337, "answered": null, "resp": "Validity generally means being well-founded or measuring up to the relevant standard; its precise meaning depends on context.\nFINAL: Validity is the extent to which something is sound or justified: in logic, an argument is valid if its conclusion must be true whenever its premises are true; in measurement, validity concerns whether evidence supports interpreting a measure as assessing what it is intended to assess.", "final": "Validity is the extent to which something is sound or justified: in logic, an argument is valid if its conclusion must be true whenever its premises are true; in measurement, validity concerns whether evidence supports interpreting a measure as assessing what it is intended to assess."}
{"qid": 338, "answered": null, "resp": "A high heritability index means that genetic differences account for a large proportion of the variation in a trait within the population and environment studied. It does not mean the trait is fixed or unaffected by environmental influences.\nFINAL: Much of the variation in the trait among individuals in that population is attributable to genetic differences.", "final": "Much of the variation in the trait among individuals in that population is attributable to genetic differences."}
{"qid": 339, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: Conduct disorder involves a persistent pattern of violating others\u2019 rights or major age-appropriate rules. Its behaviors fall into four major categories:\n1. **Aggression toward people or animals:** Bullying, threatening, fighting, using weapons, or showing physical cruelty.\n2. **Destruction of property:** Deliberately damaging others\u2019 property, including vandalism or fire-setting.\n3. **Deceitfulness or theft:** Repeated lying to obtain benefits or avoid obligations, stealing, or breaking into homes, buildings, or cars.\n4. **Serious violations of rules:** Repeatedly breaking major rules, such as running away, staying out at night despite parental prohibitions, or persistent truancy.\n\nThese are behavioral categories, not four separate diagnostic subtypes.", "final": "Conduct disorder involves a persistent pattern of violating others\u2019 rights or major age-appropriate rules. Its behaviors fall into four major categories:"}
{"qid": 340, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: Bandura (1969) identified three behavior-control systems: **(1) Stimulus control**\u2014behavior is regulated by antecedent environmental cues or events; **(2) Reinforcement control**\u2014behavior is regulated by its consequences, such as rewards or punishments; and **(3) Cognitive control**\u2014behavior is regulated by internal symbolic processes, such as thoughts, expectations, and self-directed rules.", "final": "Bandura (1969) identified three behavior-control systems: **(1) Stimulus control**\u2014behavior is regulated by antecedent environmental cues or events; **(2) Reinforcement control**\u2014behavior is regulated by its consequences, such as rewards or punishments; and **(3) Cognitive control**\u2014behavior is regulated by internal symbolic processes, such as thoughts, expectations, and self-directed rules."}
{"qid": 341, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: According to Matina Horner, the motive to avoid success is a tendency to feel anxious about achievement because success\u2014especially in traditionally male domains\u2014is expected to bring negative consequences, such as social rejection or being seen as unfeminine. She studied it using a projective story-writing task: women completed a story beginning with \u201cAnne\u201d discovering she was at the top of her medical-school class, while men received the same prompt about \u201cJohn\u201d; women\u2019s stories more often anticipated rejection, unhappiness, or other negative consequences, which Horner interpreted as evidence of fear of success.", "final": "According to Matina Horner, the motive to avoid success is a tendency to feel anxious about achievement because success\u2014especially in traditionally male domains\u2014is expected to bring negative consequences, such as social rejection or being seen as unfeminine. She studied it using a projective story-writing task: women completed a story beginning with \u201cAnne\u201d discovering she was at the top of her medical-school class, while men received the same prompt about \u201cJohn\u201d; women\u2019s stories more often anticipated rejection, unhappiness, or other negative consequences, which Horner interpreted as evidence of fear of success."}
{"qid": 342, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: Three contrasting explanations of anxiety are **biological, psychodynamic, and learning-based**. The **biological explanation** attributes anxiety to inherited vulnerability and altered functioning of brain threat systems and neurotransmitters; it helps explain physiological symptoms and familial patterns, but cannot alone explain why particular situations become threatening. The **psychodynamic explanation** views anxiety as a signal of unconscious conflict\u2014between unacceptable impulses and internal prohibitions, for example\u2014which triggers defenses such as repression or displacement; it emphasizes emotional history, but its claims are difficult to test scientifically. The **learning-based explanation** proposes that anxiety develops through conditioning, observation, or threatening information, while avoidance maintains it because escaping a feared situation brings immediate relief; it explains specific fears and supports exposure therapy, but does not fully account for anxiety without an identifiable learning history. These accounts are not mutually exclusive: biological vulnerability, internal conflict, and learning may interact.", "final": "Three contrasting explanations of anxiety are **biological, psychodynamic, and learning-based**. The **biological explanation** attributes anxiety to inherited vulnerability and altered functioning of brain threat systems and neurotransmitters; it helps explain physiological symptoms and familial patterns, but cannot alone explain why particular situations become threatening. The **psychodynamic explanation** views anxiety as a signal of unconscious conflict\u2014between unacceptable impulses and internal prohibitions, for example\u2014which triggers defenses such as repression or displacement; it emphasizes emotional history, but its claims are difficult to test scientifically. The **learning-based explanation** proposes that anxiety develops through conditioning, observation, or threatening information, while avoidance maintains it because escaping a feared situation brings immediate relief; it explains specific fears and supports exposure therapy, but does not fully account for anxiety without an identifiable learning history. These accounts are not mutually exclusive: biological vulnerability, internal conflict, and learning may interact."}
{"qid": 343, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: Hypothesis testing is a statistical method that uses sample data to assess whether there is sufficient evidence to reject a stated claim about a population (the null hypothesis) in favor of an alternative hypothesis, at a chosen significance level.", "final": "Hypothesis testing is a statistical method that uses sample data to assess whether there is sufficient evidence to reject a stated claim about a population (the null hypothesis) in favor of an alternative hypothesis, at a chosen significance level."}
{"qid": 344, "answered": null, "resp": "The standard error of measurement describes the variability of scores due to measurement error. Assuming normally distributed errors, about 68% of observed scores fall within one standard error of the true score.\nFINAL: An observed score will fall within \u00b115 points of the true score about 68% of the time, assuming normally distributed measurement errors.", "final": "An observed score will fall within \u00b115 points of the true score about 68% of the time, assuming normally distributed measurement errors."}
