{"qid": 410, "answered": null, "resp": "FINAL: The three basic goals of psychology are to **understand, predict, and influence behavior and mental processes**. **Understanding** involves describing what people think, feel, and do and explaining why; **prediction** involves anticipating how people will respond under particular conditions; and **influence or control** involves applying psychological knowledge ethically to change behavior, solve problems, and improve well-being.", "final": "The three basic goals of psychology are to **understand, predict, and influence behavior and mental processes**. **Understanding** involves describing what people think, feel, and do and explaining why; **prediction** involves anticipating how people will respond under particular conditions; and **influence or control** involves applying psychological knowledge ethically to change behavior, solve problems, and improve well-being."}
{"qid": 411, "answered": null, "resp": "Freud described an instinct (or drive) in terms of its bodily origin, motivating force, goal, and means of satisfaction.\nFINAL: The four chief characteristics are **source** (bodily origin), **pressure or impetus** (strength of the demand), **aim** (satisfaction through reducing tension), and **object** (the person or thing through which satisfaction is achieved).", "final": "The four chief characteristics are **source** (bodily origin), **pressure or impetus** (strength of the demand), **aim** (satisfaction through reducing tension), and **object** (the person or thing through which satisfaction is achieved)."}
{"qid": 412, "answered": null, "resp": "These theories describe contributing mechanisms rather than proven, complete explanations of depression; textbook classifications vary.\nFINAL: **1. Monoamine (biogenic amine) theory:** Depression involves reduced or dysregulated signaling by serotonin, norepinephrine, and sometimes dopamine in circuits governing mood, motivation, and reward. Antidepressants\u2019 effects on these transmitters support their involvement, but depression is not simply a demonstrated \u201cchemical deficiency.\u201d **2. Stress\u2013neuroendocrine/neuroplasticity theory:** Chronic stress can dysregulate the hypothalamic\u2013pituitary\u2013adrenal (HPA) axis and cortisol secretion, impairing mood-regulating brain circuits. Related reductions in brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) and synaptic plasticity may contribute; some textbooks identify this latter component separately as the **neurotrophic hypothesis**, pairing it with the monoamine theory.", "final": "**1. Monoamine (biogenic amine) theory:** Depression involves reduced or dysregulated signaling by serotonin, norepinephrine, and sometimes dopamine in circuits governing mood, motivation, and reward. Antidepressants\u2019 effects on these transmitters support their involvement, but depression is not simply a demonstrated \u201cchemical deficiency.\u201d **2. Stress\u2013neuroendocrine/neuroplasticity theory:** Chronic stress can dysregulate the hypothalamic\u2013pituitary\u2013adrenal (HPA) axis and cortisol secretion, impairing mood-regulating brain circuits. Related reductions in brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) and synaptic plasticity may contribute; some textbooks identify this latter component separately as the **neurotrophic hypothesis**, pairing it with the monoamine theory."}
{"qid": 413, "answered": null, "resp": "\u201cFunctional psychoses\u201d is an older term for psychotic disorders without an identifiable organic cause; it does not mean they are purely psychological. They are generally understood to arise from interacting biological vulnerabilities and psychological and environmental influences.\nFINAL: A combination of genetic and neurobiological predispositions, psychological factors, and environmental stressors\u2014not a single established cause.", "final": "A combination of genetic and neurobiological predispositions, psychological factors, and environmental stressors\u2014not a single established cause."}
